// Configuration that varies by environment or must not be committed.
//
// Secrets.xcconfig holds your real values and is gitignored.
// Secrets.xcconfig.example is committed and records which keys must exist.
// Add a key to both with /add-secret, never by hand — but paste any credential
// in yourself. A value typed into an AI agent's prompt lives in that transcript,
// and in every log along the way, long after you rotate the key.
//
// API_BASE_URL below is the one key name this template itself reads — AppEnvironment
// resolves it, the app target's Info.plist carries $(API_BASE_URL), and
// check_secrets.sh checks its scheme and host. Its value here is a placeholder, and
// the commented-out API_KEY line is an example of the shape a second key takes, not
// a key your project needs. Name your own keys whatever your project calls them.
//
// URLs need the $() escape — `//` starts a comment here, so write
// https:/$()/host. Quotes and backslashes do not work; see
// docs/CODING_STANDARDS.md for why. A blank value is a failure, not a default:
// run Scripts/check_secrets.sh to catch one before the simulator does.

// Base URL for every request, read by the composition root — never by a Service.
API_BASE_URL = https:/$()/api.staging.example.com

// A key compiled into the binary is extractable from the IPA. Add one only if the
// vendor allows a public client key; anything sensitive stays server-side.
// Uncommenting this alone does nothing — /add-secret does the Info.plist and
// AppEnvironment wiring that makes a key readable.
// API_KEY =
